Changes made for march:

  * The configuration is written in Lua, not hyprlang. Hyprland deprecated
    hyprlang in 0.55 and will drop it entirely a release or two later; the .conf
    files were translated by hand, since Omarchy has not migrated and has no Lua
    configuration to vendor. The behaviour is meant to be identical.

  * Bindings that invoke Omarchy's own binaries (omarchy-menu, omarchy-launch-*,
    omarchy-toggle-*, omarchy-capture-*, voxtype, ...) are either repointed at
    equivalents packaged for Arch Linux ARM or dropped. None of Omarchy's own
    packages are built for aarch64, so keeping them would produce shortcuts that
    silently do nothing.

  * The app launcher is fuzzel rather than walker: walker and its elephant
    backend have no aarch64 package.

  * Blur, shadows and animations are disabled. march's VMs have no GPU
    passthrough, so Mesa renders in software via llvmpipe and these effects cost
    far more than they are worth.

  * Laptop-only bindings (lid switch, touchpad toggles, keyboard backlight) are
    removed, having no meaning in a virtual machine.

  * Theme-switching machinery is omitted; march ships a single look.

  * Omarchy's menu and bar are Quickshell plugins, and quickshell has no aarch64
    package. The menu tree is rebuilt as a table read by fuzzel (bin/march-menu)
    and the bar's widgets as waybar modules fed by bin/march-bar. Branches
    needing hardware that QEMU's virt machine does not have — bluetooth,
    battery, display brightness, weather, dictation — are dropped, as is
    Suspend: the guest can enter it, but nothing on the host side sends the
    QMP system_wakeup that would bring it back.

  * Screen recording uses wf-recorder rather than gpu-screen-recorder, which
    needs a hardware video encoder that virtio-gpu does not expose.

  * hypridle is not installed. A guest window sits inside a host that already
    locks itself.

  * The browser is Google Chrome rather than Omarchy's Chromium, unpacked from
    Google's own aarch64 .deb because no Arch package carries one.
